ab;}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;}td,th{border:1px solid #ccc;padding:8px;}th{background:#e8eaf6;}p{margin:8px 0;}</style></head><body><h1>cybersecurity_incident_summary_report_202603.docx</h1><p><strong><em>Cybersecurity_incident_summary_report_202603</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>VS.Code-Copilot-BMAD Integration</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>Prompt</em></strong></p><p>I usually use Clause Sonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.3-Codex.</p><p>These two are Agents incorporated into github copilot plan included in the list of agents.</p><p>What i really want is that whichever agent i use, the agent will use the BMAD architecture as the persona and hand it off to another persona while still being the agent that i selected.</p><p>Is that possible? if yes, tell me the steps to make it happen. and also, i want to install BMAD in a single path and then reference it everytime i make a chat.</p><p>Make the steps more understandable, complete instructions, meaning i should be able to understand completely what file i need to create, where to save it, how to use it and so on.</p><p>Yes, with one important correction:</p><p><strong>Claude Sonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.3 Codex are models, not the BMAD personas themselves.</strong> In VS Code Copilot, you can keep using whichever model you select, while a <strong>custom agent + instructions</strong> make that model behave like a BMAD orchestrator. VS Code treats <strong>models</strong>, <strong>custom agents</strong>, and <strong>instructions</strong> as separate layers, and custom agents are defined in .agent.md files while instructions can be applied globally or per workspace. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><p>The practical result is:</p><ul><li>you keep <strong>Claude Sonnet</strong> or <strong>GPT-5.3 Codex</strong> as the model</li><li>you create <strong>one BMAD orchestrator agent</strong></li><li>that orchestrator <strong>routes internally</strong> to analyst / architect / dev / QA logic</li><li>you do <strong>not</strong> have to name the BMAD persona on every prompt</li></ul><p>The one limitation is that <strong>true invisible runtime handoff</strong> is not fully the same as a hidden multi-agent runtime. In VS Code, handoffs are a supported workflow feature, but they are presented as guided transitions between agents. If you want the selected top-level agent to stay the same every time, the most reliable setup is a <strong>single BMAD orchestrator agent that emulates the specialist personas internally</strong> instead of depending on visible agent switching. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><h2><a id=\"_dy2xlejik5rm\"></a><strong>What you should build</strong></h2><p>Use this design:</p><p><strong>Model layer</strong></p><ul><li>Claude Sonnet 4.6 or GPT-5.3 Codex</li></ul><p><strong>Behavior layer</strong></p><ul><li>one custom agent called bmad-orchestrator</li></ul><p><strong>Global memory layer</strong></p><ul><li>user-level instruction files in ~/.copilot/instructions</li></ul><p><strong>Per-project memory layer</strong></p><ul><li>repo files like .github/copilot-instructions.md, .github/instructions/*.instructions.md, and optionally AGENTS.md</li></ul><p>That gives you BMAD behavior across all chats, while still letting you choose whichever model you want. VS Code supports user-level custom agents in ~/.copilot/agents and user-level instructions in ~/.copilot/instructions, both reusable across workspaces. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><h1><a id=\"_x440hly3rsdr\"></a><strong>What is actually possible</strong></h1><h2><a id=\"_59m05wmx820w\"></a><strong>Yes, this part is possible</strong></h2><p>You can make Copilot behave like this:</p><p>ΓÇ£Whatever model I pick, use BMAD as the default operating system. Automatically classify the request, act as analyst / architect / dev / QA as needed, and only expose one top-level agent to me.ΓÇ¥</p><p>That is the best-supported setup.</p><h2><a id=\"_89a5irxde4s8\"></a><strong>This part is only partially possible</strong></h2><p>You asked for:</p><p>ΓÇ£hand it off to another persona while still being the agent that I selectedΓÇ¥</p><p>You can absolutely make the selected agent <strong>internally switch roles</strong> and tell you which BMAD role it is currently using.</p><p>What is less reliable is a fully hidden, literal subagent runtime where the UI never reflects any transition at all. VS Code supports handoffs, but those are designed as guided workflows between agents. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><p>So the correct implementation is:</p><ul><li>keep <strong>one visible agent</strong></li><li>let that one agent <strong>simulate BMAD role handoff internally</strong></li><li>optionally add real specialist agents later if you want visible handoff buttons</li></ul><p>That gives you the behavior you want without depending on fragile UI mechanics.</p><h1><a id=\"_premr1evz03d\"></a><strong>Best setup for your goal</strong></h1><h2><a id=\"_7t0gfjvzrqha\"></a><strong>Part 1: Install BMAD once in one central folder</strong></h2><p>Create one central BMAD folder on your machine.</p><h3><a id=\"_51y01dg2i23j\"></a><strong>Windows</strong></h3><p><strong>ε░â</strong>C:\\Users\\YOUR-USERNAME\\.bmad-method\\</p><h3><a id=\"_oatbhjjt7xw\"></a>ε░é<strong>macOS / Linux</strong></h3><p><strong>ε░â</strong>~/.bmad-method/</p><p>ε░éThen install or clone BMAD there.</p><h3><a id=\"_rg84p2pplps0\"></a><strong>Option A ΓÇö easiest: clone the BMAD repo</strong></h3><p><strong>ε░â</strong>git clone https://github.com/bmad-code-org/BMAD-METHOD.git ~/.bmad-method</p><h3><a id=\"_x73tto2a7xe9\"></a>ε░é<strong>Option B ΓÇö run the official installer into a working directory</strong></h3><p>BMADΓÇÖs quick start uses:</p><p>ε░ânpx bmad-method install</p><p>ε░éand also supports non-interactive installation with --directory. (<a href=\"https://github.com/bmad-code-org/BMAD-METHOD\">GitHub</a>)</p><p>Example:</p><p>ε░ânpx bmad-method install --directory ~/.bmad-method --yes</p><p>ε░éBMAD also recently added a dedicated GitHub Copilot installer that generates .agent.md, .prompt.md, and project configuration. (<a href=\"https://github.com/bmad-code-org/BMAD-METHOD/releases?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">GitHub</a>)</p><h2><a id=\"_shett8a9i6gu\"></a><strong>Important note</strong></h2><p>Copilot does <strong>not</strong> automatically read arbitrary folders on every chat just because BMAD is installed there.</p><p>So installing BMAD in one folder is useful as your <strong>source of truth</strong>, but to make it active in chat, you must place the files Copilot actually recognizes in its supported locations:</p><ul><li>~/.copilot/agents</li><li>~/.copilot/instructions</li></ul><p>That is the key detail. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><h1><a id=\"_uj1iney6sauc\"></a><strong>Part 2: Create one global BMAD orchestrator agent</strong></h1><p>Create this folder if it does not exist:</p><h3><a id=\"_l2dm1asekdnh\"></a><strong>Windows</strong></h3><p><strong>ε░â</strong>C:\\Users\\YOUR-USERNAME\\.copilot\\agents\\</p><h3><a id=\"_kx3y32h0kw2i\"></a>ε░é<strong>macOS / Linux</strong></h3><p><strong>ε░â</strong>~/.copilot/agents/</p><p>ε░éVS Code supports user-level custom agents in ~/.copilot/agents. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><p>Now create this file:</p><p>ε░â~/.copilot/agents/bmad-orchestrator.agent.md</p><p>ε░éPut this content inside:</p><p>ε░â---</p><p>name: bmad-orchestrator</p><p>description: Default BMAD orchestrator. Use BMAD workflow automatically while keeping the user\'s selected model. Internally route each request to analyst, architect, developer, QA, or multi-role execution without requiring the user to name an agent.</p><p>tools: [\'codebase\', \'edit\', \'search\', \'runCommands\', \'terminal\', \'test\']</p><p>modelPreferences:</p><p>  temperature: 0.2</p><p>---</p><p># Role</p><p>You are the BMAD Orchestrator for all coding chats.</p><p>The user may select any available Copilot model, including Claude Sonnet or GPT-5.x Codex.  </p><p>You must keep using the selected model, but apply BMAD operating logic to every request.</p><p># Core behavior</p><p>For every user request:</p><p>1. Classify the request into one or more BMAD roles:</p><p>   - Analyst</p><p>   - Architect</p><p>   - Developer</p><p>   - QA</p><p>   - Multi-role</p><p>2. Do not ask the user which BMAD role to use unless the request is genuinely blocked by missing business direction.</p><p>3. Internally hand off to the correct BMAD persona while remaining the same visible top-level agent.</p><p>4. When multiple roles are needed, execute them in this order unless the task clearly requires another order:</p><p>   - Analyst</p><p>   - Architect</p><p>   - Developer</p><p>   - QA</p><p>5. Keep the response unified. Do not force the user to switch agents.</p><p># Routing rules</p><p>Use these rules automatically:</p><p>- Analyst:</p><p>  requirements clarification, scoping, product thinking, user stories, acceptance criteria, feature framing, business logic discovery</p><p>- Architect:</p><p>  system design, folder structure, API design, database design, integration design, security architecture, tradeoffs, technical strategy</p><p>- Developer:</p><p>  code generation, refactoring, debugging, implementation, migrations, tests, scripts, setup</p><p>- QA:</p><p>  validation, edge cases, test plans, regression risks, release readiness, traceability, nonfunctional checks</p><p># Execution rules</p><p>- For small requests, respond directly using the appropriate BMAD persona internally.</p><p>- For medium or large requests, briefly classify the work, then perform the BMAD sequence automatically.</p><p>- Prefer using existing repository instructions and artifacts before inventing new standards.</p><p>- If the repository contains planning artifacts, read and follow them.</p><p>- If no artifacts exist, infer the minimal necessary structure and proceed.</p><p># Output format</p><p>At the start of each answer, include:</p><p>- BMAD Route:</p><p>- Active Role(s):</p><p>- What I checked:</p><p>- Next action:</p><p>Then continue with the actual work.</p><p># Guardrails</p><p>- Stay concise unless the task is architectural or planning-heavy.</p><p>- Do not mention hidden internal orchestration unless it helps the user.</p><p>- Keep one visible agent identity: bmad-orchestrator.</p><p>- Preserve the selected model. Do not tell the user to change models unless required.</p><h2><a id=\"_co54fypqory9\"></a>ε░é<strong>What this does</strong></h2><p>This file creates a <strong>single visible BMAD agent</strong>. You still choose Claude Sonnet or GPT-5.3 Codex as your model, but the selected model now operates under BMAD orchestration logic. VS Code custom agents are exactly for persistent personas, tool restrictions, and specialized workflows. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><h1><a id=\"_7wzfj24pqwt7\"></a><strong>Part 3: Create global BMAD instruction files</strong></h1><p>Now create this folder:</p><h3><a id=\"_9ujvmefmw0k4\"></a><strong>Windows</strong></h3><p><strong>ε░â</strong>C:\\Users\\YOUR-USERNAME\\.copilot\\instructions\\</p><h3><a id=\"_ftp94vcyq02c\"></a>ε░é<strong>macOS / Linux</strong></h3><p><strong>ε░â</strong>~/.copilot/instructions/</p><p>ε░éVS Code supports user-level instruction files in ~/.copilot/instructions, and these apply across all workspaces. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><p>Create this file:</p><p>ε░â~/.copilot/instructions/00-bmad-global.instructions.md</p><p>ε░éPut this inside:</p><p>ε░â---</p><p>name: BMAD Global Rules</p><p>description: Always apply BMAD reasoning across all chats and repositories</p><p>applyTo: \"**\"</p><p>---</p><p># BMAD global behavior</p><p>- Use BMAD reasoning by default on all coding and app-building requests.</p><p>- Automatically classify the task as analyst, architect, developer, QA, or multi-role.</p><p>- Prefer internal routing over asking the user which persona to use.</p><p>- Use structured outputs when the task involves architecture, planning, or implementation.</p><p>- For app-building tasks, think in this order: requirements, architecture, implementation, validation.</p><p>- Do not skip architecture when the request changes application structure, APIs, persistence, auth, security, or deployment.</p><p>- For bug fixes, first identify root cause, then propose the smallest safe change, then define validation steps.</p><p>- For feature work, state assumptions, dependencies, affected files, and validation steps.</p><p>- Preserve consistency with repository instructions and AGENTS.md when present.</p><h2><a id=\"_h11fs7nd39rh\"></a>ε░é<strong>What this file does</strong></h2><p>This is your <strong>always-on BMAD behavior</strong> across all projects. VS Code automatically applies user-level instructions across workspaces. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><h1><a id=\"_zci2ehcvw7n5\"></a><strong>Part 4: Add a project-level file in every repo</strong></h1><p>Global files make BMAD available everywhere, but each repository should still define its own local rules.</p><p>At the root of each repo, create:</p><p>ε░â.github/copilot-instructions.md</p><p>ε░éGitHub and VS Code both recognize this file as repository-wide custom instructions. (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/copilot/customizing-copilot/adding-custom-instructions-for-github-copilot\">GitHub Docs</a>)</p><p>Use this content:</p><p>ε░â# Repository BMAD rules</p><p>This repository uses BMAD as the default development method.</p><p>For every request:</p><p>- consult existing code and docs before proposing changes</p><p>- keep changes small, testable, and traceable</p><p>- for non-trivial changes, think through analysis, architecture, implementation, and QA</p><p>- maintain consistency with the project structure and naming conventions</p><p>- when creating new modules, follow the existing folder and dependency patterns</p><p>- when proposing architecture changes, explain tradeoffs briefly</p><p>- when implementing, list affected files and validation steps</p><h2><a id=\"_g4mvdkxli80z\"></a>ε░é<strong>Why you still need this</strong></h2><p>Your user-level files make BMAD global, but .github/copilot-instructions.md gives each repo its own local constraints. Repository instructions are a first-class supported mechanism in Copilot. (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/copilot/customizing-copilot/adding-custom-instructions-for-github-copilot\">GitHub Docs</a>)</p><h1><a id=\"_njj9jfjuzg4u\"></a><strong>Part 5: Optional per-language and per-folder BMAD rules</strong></h1><p>If you want stronger behavior by stack, create path-specific instruction files inside:</p><p>ε░â.github/instructions/</p><p>ε░éBoth GitHub Docs and VS Code support *.instructions.md files there, with applyTo globs. (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/copilot/customizing-copilot/adding-custom-instructions-for-github-copilot\">GitHub Docs</a>)</p><p>Example for React:</p><p>ε░â.github/instructions/react.instructions.md</p><p>ε░é</p><p>ε░â---</p><p>name: React BMAD Rules</p><p>description: Apply BMAD front-end guidance for React files</p><p>applyTo: \"**/*.tsx,**/*.ts\"</p><p>---</p><p>- Prefer component decomposition before large components.</p><p>- Separate view logic from business logic where practical.</p><p>- When adding a feature, identify state shape, side effects, and validation requirements first.</p><p>- Add tests for critical behavior and edge cases.</p><p>ε░éExample for backend:</p><p>ε░â.github/instructions/backend.instructions.md</p><p>ε░é</p><p>ε░â---</p><p>name: Backend BMAD Rules</p><p>description: Apply BMAD backend guidance</p><p>applyTo: \"src/api/**,src/server/**,**/*.py,**/*.go\"</p><p>---</p><p>- Define input/output contracts before writing handlers.</p><p>- Validate auth, error handling, and logging impacts for every endpoint change.</p><p>- Prefer small services with explicit dependencies.</p><p>- Add validation and regression checks for business-critical changes.</p><p>ε░é</p><h1><a id=\"_eky79fvg1vpa\"></a><strong>Part 6: How to use it in VS Code</strong></h1><h2><a id=\"_q5qikamfkd66\"></a><strong>Step A ΓÇö restart VS Code</strong></h2><p>After creating the files, restart VS Code.</p><h2><a id=\"_gnqev9h21qlr\"></a><strong>Step B ΓÇö verify Copilot sees your customizations</strong></h2><p>Open the Command Palette and run:</p><p>ε░âChat: Open Chat Customizations</p><p>ε░éor open the Chat view, then use the configuration gear. VS Code exposes custom agents and instruction files through the chat customization system. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><h2><a id=\"_rg7gyf7b4g1b\"></a><strong>Step C ΓÇö choose your model</strong></h2><p>Pick:</p><ul><li>Claude Sonnet 4.6, or</li><li>GPT-5.3 Codex</li></ul><h2><a id=\"_hw4yckv5um7l\"></a><strong>Step D ΓÇö choose your agent</strong></h2><p>In the Agents dropdown, choose:</p><p>ε░âbmad-orchestrator</p><p>ε░éNow your selected <strong>model</strong> stays the brain, while <strong>bmad-orchestrator</strong> is the behavior shell.</p><p>That is the combination you want.</p><h1><a id=\"_36kwf5vpl8jy\"></a><strong>Part 7: How your prompts should look</strong></h1><p>Once this is set up, your prompts can stay natural.</p><p>Examples:</p><p>ε░âBuild a CRM MVP with auth, customer records, tasks, and activity history.</p><p>ε░é</p><p>ε░âRefactor the billing module and reduce duplicate service logic.</p><p>ε░é</p><p>ε░âAdd role-based access control to this app.</p><p>ε░é</p><p>ε░âFind why the checkout flow intermittently fails and fix it.</p><p>ε░éYou do <strong>not</strong> need to say:</p><ul><li>ΓÇ£use the architectΓÇ¥</li><li>ΓÇ£use the analystΓÇ¥</li><li>ΓÇ£switch to QAΓÇ¥</li></ul><p>The orchestrator should infer that.</p><h1><a id=\"_4tge422pvt0c\"></a><strong>Part 8: If you want BMAD loaded from one single path only</strong></h1><p>This is the honest answer:</p><p><strong>Not fully, by itself.</strong></p><p>A central BMAD folder like ~/.bmad-method/ is excellent as your source repository, but CopilotΓÇÖs supported always-on mechanisms are still the files it scans in recognized locations such as:</p><ul><li>~/.copilot/agents</li><li>~/.copilot/instructions</li><li>repo .github/copilot-instructions.md</li><li>repo .github/instructions/*.instructions.md</li><li>AGENTS.md in workspace roots (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</li></ul><p>So the correct pattern is:</p><h2><a id=\"_49bqlwr1gqxt\"></a><strong>Recommended pattern</strong></h2><ul><li>keep the <strong>full BMAD package</strong> in ~/.bmad-method/</li><li>keep a <strong>thin global agent file</strong> in ~/.copilot/agents</li><li>keep <strong>thin global BMAD rules</strong> in ~/.copilot/instructions</li><li>keep <strong>repo-specific files</strong> inside each project</li></ul><p>That is the most reliable setup.</p><h1><a id=\"_e2zd4c8eckky\"></a><strong>Part 9: If you want one-click rollout to all your future repos</strong></h1><p>Create a starter repository template with:</p><p>ε░â.github/</p><p>  copilot-instructions.md</p><p>  instructions/</p><p>    react.instructions.md</p><p>    backend.instructions.md</p><p>AGENTS.md</p><p>docs/</p><p>  architecture.md</p><p>  requirements.md</p><p>ε░éThen every new project starts from that template.</p><p>You can also keep your user-level BMAD orchestrator in ~/.copilot/agents, so it is available everywhere regardless of repo. VS Code supports user-level and workspace-level custom agents separately. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><h1><a id=\"_be7xd352yd9\"></a><strong>Part 10: A more advanced version using AGENTS.md</strong></h1><p>In each repo root, optionally add:</p><p>ε░âAGENTS.md</p><p>ε░éVS Code recognizes AGENTS.md as always-on instructions and explicitly recommends it when multiple AI agents work in the workspace. (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</p><p>Example:</p><p>ε░â# AGENTS.md</p><p>This repository uses BMAD orchestration by default.</p><p>Operating rules:</p><p>- Start by classifying the task into analysis, architecture, implementation, or QA.</p><p>- Use the smallest sufficient BMAD workflow.</p><p>- Keep outputs traceable from request to implementation.</p><p>- For medium or large work, produce a mini-plan before changing files.</p><p>- Do not introduce architectural changes silently.</p><p>- Always define validation steps.</p><p>ε░éThis helps when multiple agents or models are used in the same project.</p><h1><a id=\"_sbdusi12wpkr\"></a><strong>Part 11: Your exact target behavior</strong></h1><p>What you want is best implemented like this:</p><h2><a id=\"_igb7chm60kqb\"></a><strong>Final working setup</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>Install BMAD centrally</strong><ul><li>~/.bmad-method/</li></ul></li><li><strong>Create one global orchestrator</strong><ul><li>~/.copilot/agents/bmad-orchestrator.agent.md</li></ul></li><li><strong>Create one global BMAD instruction file</strong><ul><li>~/.copilot/instructions/00-bmad-global.instructions.md</li></ul></li><li><strong>Add repo instructions in each project</strong><ul><li>.github/copilot-instructions.md</li></ul></li><li><strong>Optionally add stack-specific rules</strong><ul><li>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md</li></ul></li><li><strong>In VS Code chat</strong><ul><li>choose model: Claude Sonnet 4.6 or GPT-5.3 Codex</li><li>choose agent: bmad-orchestrator</li></ul></li></ol><p>That will make whichever model you use behave in BMAD style, with automatic internal role routing.</p><h1><a id=\"_g1dwpq3h3pq5\"></a><strong>Part 12: Limitations you should know</strong></h1><p>A few things will still be true:</p><ul><li>custom instructions generally affect <strong>chat requests</strong>, not normal inline autocomplete typing in the editor (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</li><li>real cross-agent handoffs in VS Code are supported, but they are still a visible workflow concept rather than a completely hidden runtime router (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-agents\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</li><li>repository instructions can override or refine behavior, while user-level personal instructions have higher priority than repo-level ones (<a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\">Visual Studio Code</a>)</li></ul><p>So this setup is extremely close to what you want, but the most reliable version is still <strong>one orchestrator agent that internally performs BMAD routing</strong>.</p><h1><a id=\"_703qopun96qb\"></a><strong>Fast checklist</strong></h1><p>Create these exact paths:</p><p>ε░â~/.bmad-method/</p><p>~/.copilot/agents/bmad-orchestrator.agent.md</p><p>~/.copilot/instructions/00-bmad-global.instructions.md</p><p>&lt;each-repo&gt;/.github/copilot-instructions.md</p><p>&lt;each-repo&gt;/.github/instructions/react.instructions.md</p><p>&lt;each-repo&gt;/.github/instructions/backend.instructions.md</p><p>&lt;each-repo&gt;/AGENTS.md</p><p>ε░éThen in VS Code:</p><ol><li>restart</li><li>open Copilot Chat</li><li>select your model</li><li>select bmad-orchestrator</li><li>start chatting normally</li></ol><p>If you want, I can turn this into a <strong>copy-paste BMAD starter pack</strong> with all file contents for a TypeScript/React + API project.</p></body></html>','text/html','Cybersecurity_incident_summary_report_202603\n\nVS.Code-Copilot-BMAD Integration\n\nPrompt\n\nI usually use Clause Sonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.3-Codex.\n\nThese two are Agents incorporated into github copilot plan included in the list of agents.\n\nWhat i really want is that whichever agent i use, the agent will use the BMAD architecture as the persona and hand it off to another persona while still being the agent that i selected.\n\nIs that possible? if yes, tell me the steps to make it happen. and also, i want to install BMAD in a single path and then reference it everytime i make a chat.\n\nMake the steps more understandable, complete instructions, meaning i should be able to understand completely what file i need to create, where to save it, how to use it and so on.\n\n\n\nYes, with one important correction:\n\nClaude Sonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.3 Codex are models, not the BMAD personas themselves. In VS Code Copilot, you can keep using whichever model you select, while a custom agent + instructions make that model behave like a BMAD orchestrator. VS Code treats models, custom agents, and instructions as separate layers, and custom agents are defined in .agent.md files while instructions can be applied globally or per workspace. (Visual Studio Code)\n\nThe practical result is:\n\nyou keep Claude Sonnet or GPT-5.3 Codex as the model\n\nyou create one BMAD orchestrator agent\n\nthat orchestrator routes internally to analyst / architect / dev / QA logic\n\nyou do not have to name the BMAD persona on every prompt\n\nThe one limitation is that true invisible runtime handoff is not fully the same as a hidden multi-agent runtime. In VS Code, handoffs are a supported workflow feature, but they are presented as guided transitions between agents. If you want the selected top-level agent to stay the same every time, the most reliable setup is a single BMAD orchestrator agent that emulates the specialist personas internally instead of depending on visible agent switching. (Visual Studio Code)\n\nWhat you should build\n\nUse this design:\n\nModel layer\n\nClaude Sonnet 4.6 or GPT-5.3 Codex\n\nBehavior layer\n\none custom agent called bmad-orchestrator\n\nGlobal memory layer\n\nuser-level instruction files in ~/.copilot/instructions\n\nPer-project memory layer\n\nrepo files like .github/copilot-instructions.md, .github/instructions/*.instructions.md, and optionally AGENTS.md\n\nThat gives you BMAD behavior across all chats, while still letting you choose whichever model you want. VS Code supports user-level custom agents in ~/.copilot/agents and user-level instructions in ~/.copilot/instructions, both reusable across workspaces. (Visual Studio Code)\n\n\n\nWhat is actually possible\n\nYes, this part is possible\n\nYou can make Copilot behave like this:\n\nΓÇ£Whatever model I pick, use BMAD as the default operating system. Automatically classify the request, act as analyst / architect / dev / QA as needed, and only expose one top-level agent to me.ΓÇ¥\n\nThat is the best-supported setup.\n\nThis part is only partially possible\n\nYou asked for:\n\nΓÇ£hand it off to another persona while still being the agent that I selectedΓÇ¥\n\nYou can absolutely make the selected agent internally switch roles and tell you which BMAD role it is currently using.\n\nWhat is less reliable is a fully hidden, literal subagent runtime where the UI never reflects any transition at all. VS Code supports handoffs, but those are designed as guided workflows between agents. (Visual Studio Code)\n\nSo the correct implementation is:\n\nkeep one visible agent\n\nlet that one agent simulate BMAD role handoff internally\n\noptionally add real specialist agents later if you want visible handoff buttons\n\nThat gives you the behavior you want without depending on fragile UI mechanics.\n\n\n\nBest setup for your goal\n\nPart 1: Install BMAD once in one central folder\n\nCreate one central BMAD folder on your machine.\n\nWindows\n\nε░âC:\\Users\\YOUR-USERNAME\\.bmad-method\\\n\nε░émacOS / Linux\n\nε░â~/.bmad-method/\n\nε░éThen install or clone BMAD there.\n\nOption A ΓÇö easiest: clone the BMAD repo\n\nε░âgit clone https://github.com/bmad-code-org/BMAD-METHOD.git ~/.bmad-method\n\nε░éOption B ΓÇö run the official installer into a working directory\n\nBMADΓÇÖs quick start uses:\n\nε░ânpx bmad-method install\n\nε░éand also supports non-interactive installation with --directory. (GitHub)\n\nExample:\n\nε░ânpx bmad-method install --directory ~/.bmad-method --yes\n\nε░éBMAD also recently added a dedicated GitHub Copilot installer that generates .agent.md, .prompt.md, and project configuration. (GitHub)\n\nImportant note\n\nCopilot does not automatically read arbitrary folders on every chat just because BMAD is installed there.\n\nSo installing BMAD in one folder is useful as your source of truth, but to make it active in chat, you must place the files Copilot actually recognizes in its supported locations:\n\n~/.copilot/agents\n\n~/.copilot/instructions\n\nThat is the key detail. (Visual Studio Code)\n\n\n\nPart 2: Create one global BMAD orchestrator agent\n\nCreate this folder if it does not exist:\n\nWindows\n\nε░âC:\\Users\\YOUR-USERNAME\\.copilot\\agents\\\n\nε░émacOS / Linux\n\nε░â~/.copilot/agents/\n\nε░éVS Code supports user-level custom agents in ~/.copilot/agents. (Visual Studio Code)\n\nNow create this file:\n\nε░â~/.copilot/agents/bmad-orchestrator.agent.md\n\nε░éPut this content inside:\n\nε░â---\n\nname: bmad-orchestrator\n\ndescription: Default BMAD orchestrator. Use BMAD workflow automatically while keeping the user\'s selected model. Internally route each request to analyst, architect, developer, QA, or multi-role execution without requiring the user to name an agent.\n\ntools: [\'codebase\', \'edit\', \'search\', \'runCommands\', \'terminal\', \'test\']\n\nmodelPreferences:\n\n  temperature: 0.2\n\n---\n\n\n\n# Role\n\n\n\nYou are the BMAD Orchestrator for all coding chats.\n\n\n\nThe user may select any available Copilot model, including Claude Sonnet or GPT-5.x Codex.  \n\nYou must keep using the selected model, but apply BMAD operating logic to every request.\n\n\n\n# Core behavior\n\n\n\nFor every user request:\n\n\n\n1. Classify the request into one or more BMAD roles:\n\n   - Analyst\n\n   - Architect\n\n   - Developer\n\n   - QA\n\n   - Multi-role\n\n\n\n2. Do not ask the user which BMAD role to use unless the request is genuinely blocked by missing business direction.\n\n\n\n3. Internally hand off to the correct BMAD persona while remaining the same visible top-level agent.\n\n\n\n4. When multiple roles are needed, execute them in this order unless the task clearly requires another order:\n\n   - Analyst\n\n   - Architect\n\n   - Developer\n\n   - QA\n\n\n\n5. Keep the response unified. Do not force the user to switch agents.\n\n\n\n# Routing rules\n\n\n\nUse these rules automatically:\n\n\n\n- Analyst:\n\n  requirements clarification, scoping, product thinking, user stories, acceptance criteria, feature framing, business logic discovery\n\n\n\n- Architect:\n\n  system design, folder structure, API design, database design, integration design, security architecture, tradeoffs, technical strategy\n\n\n\n- Developer:\n\n  code generation, refactoring, debugging, implementation, migrations, tests, scripts, setup\n\n\n\n- QA:\n\n  validation, edge cases, test plans, regression risks, release readiness, traceability, nonfunctional checks\n\n\n\n# Execution rules\n\n\n\n- For small requests, respond directly using the appropriate BMAD persona internally.\n\n- For medium or large requests, briefly classify the work, then perform the BMAD sequence automatically.\n\n- Prefer using existing repository instructions and artifacts before inventing new standards.\n\n- If the repository contains planning artifacts, read and follow them.\n\n- If no artifacts exist, infer the minimal necessary structure and proceed.\n\n\n\n# Output format\n\n\n\nAt the start of each answer, include:\n\n\n\n- BMAD Route:\n\n- Active Role(s):\n\n- What I checked:\n\n- Next action:\n\n\n\nThen continue with the actual work.\n\n\n\n# Guardrails\n\n\n\n- Stay concise unless the task is architectural or planning-heavy.\n\n- Do not mention hidden internal orchestration unless it helps the user.\n\n- Keep one visible agent identity: bmad-orchestrator.\n\n- Preserve the selected model. Do not tell the user to change models unless required.\n\nε░éWhat this does\n\nThis file creates a single visible BMAD agent. You still choose Claude Sonnet or GPT-5.3 Codex as your model, but the selected model now operates under BMAD orchestration logic. VS Code custom agents are exactly for persistent personas, tool restrictions, and specialized workflows. (Visual Studio Code)\n\n\n\nPart 3: Create global BMAD instruction files\n\nNow create this folder:\n\nWindows\n\nε░âC:\\Users\\YOUR-USERNAME\\.copilot\\instructions\\\n\nε░émacOS / Linux\n\nε░â~/.copilot/instructions/\n\nε░éVS Code supports user-level instruction files in ~/.copilot/instructions, and these apply across all workspaces. (Visual Studio Code)\n\nCreate this file:\n\nε░â~/.copilot/instructions/00-bmad-global.instructions.md\n\nε░éPut this inside:\n\nε░â---\n\nname: BMAD Global Rules\n\ndescription: Always apply BMAD reasoning across all chats and repositories\n\napplyTo: \"**\"\n\n---\n\n\n\n# BMAD global behavior\n\n\n\n- Use BMAD reasoning by default on all coding and app-building requests.\n\n- Automatically classify the task as analyst, architect, developer, QA, or multi-role.\n\n- Prefer internal routing over asking the user which persona to use.\n\n- Use structured outputs when the task involves architecture, planning, or implementation.\n\n- For app-building tasks, think in this order: requirements, architecture, implementation, validation.\n\n- Do not skip architecture when the request changes application structure, APIs, persistence, auth, security, or deployment.\n\n- For bug fixes, first identify root cause, then propose the smallest safe change, then define validation steps.\n\n- For feature work, state assumptions, dependencies, affected files, and validation steps.\n\n- Preserve consistency with repository instructions and AGENTS.md when present.\n\nε░éWhat this file does\n\nThis is your always-on BMAD behavior across all projects. VS Code automatically applies user-level instructions across workspaces. (Visual Studio Code)\n\n\n\nPart 4: Add a project-level file in every repo\n\nGlobal files make BMAD available everywhere, but each repository should still define its own local rules.\n\nAt the root of each repo, create:\n\nε░â.github/copilot-instructions.md\n\nε░éGitHub and VS Code both recognize this file as repository-wide custom instructions. (GitHub Docs)\n\nUse this content:\n\nε░â# Repository BMAD rules\n\n\n\nThis repository uses BMAD as the default development method.\n\n\n\nFor every request:\n\n- consult existing code and docs before proposing changes\n\n- keep changes small, testable, and traceable\n\n- for non-trivial changes, think through analysis, architecture, implementation, and QA\n\n- maintain consistency with the project structure and naming conventions\n\n- when creating new modules, follow the existing folder and dependency patterns\n\n- when proposing architecture changes, explain tradeoffs briefly\n\n- when implementing, list affected files and validation steps\n\nε░éWhy you still need this\n\nYour user-level files make BMAD global, but .github/copilot-instructions.md gives each repo its own local constraints. Repository instructions are a first-class supported mechanism in Copilot. (GitHub Docs)\n\n\n\nPart 5: Optional per-language and per-folder BMAD rules\n\nIf you want stronger behavior by stack, create path-specific instruction files inside:\n\nε░â.github/instructions/\n\nε░éBoth GitHub Docs and VS Code support *.instructions.md files there, with applyTo globs. (GitHub Docs)\n\nExample for React:\n\nε░â.github/instructions/react.instructions.md\n\nε░é\n\nε░â---\n\nname: React BMAD Rules\n\ndescription: Apply BMAD front-end guidance for React files\n\napplyTo: \"**/*.tsx,**/*.ts\"\n\n---\n\n\n\n- Prefer component decomposition before large components.\n\n- Separate view logic from business logic where practical.\n\n- When adding a feature, identify state shape, side effects, and validation requirements first.\n\n- Add tests for critical behavior and edge cases.\n\nε░éExample for backend:\n\nε░â.github/instructions/backend.instructions.md\n\nε░é\n\nε░â---\n\nname: Backend BMAD Rules\n\ndescription: Apply BMAD backend guidance\n\napplyTo: \"src/api/**,src/server/**,**/*.py,**/*.go\"\n\n---\n\n\n\n- Define input/output contracts before writing handlers.\n\n- Validate auth, error handling, and logging impacts for every endpoint change.\n\n- Prefer small services with explicit dependencies.\n\n- Add validation and regression checks for business-critical changes.\n\nε░é\n\nPart 6: How to use it in VS Code\n\nStep A ΓÇö restart VS Code\n\nAfter creating the files, restart VS Code.\n\nStep B ΓÇö verify Copilot sees your customizations\n\nOpen the Command Palette and run:\n\nε░âChat: Open Chat Customizations\n\nε░éor open the Chat view, then use the configuration gear. VS Code exposes custom agents and instruction files through the chat customization system. (Visual Studio Code)\n\nStep C ΓÇö choose your model\n\nPick:\n\nClaude Sonnet 4.6, or\n\nGPT-5.3 Codex\n\nStep D ΓÇö choose your agent\n\nIn the Agents dropdown, choose:\n\nε░âbmad-orchestrator\n\nε░éNow your selected model stays the brain, while bmad-orchestrator is the behavior shell.\n\nThat is the combination you want.\n\n\n\nPart 7: How your prompts should look\n\nOnce this is set up, your prompts can stay natural.\n\nExamples:\n\nε░âBuild a CRM MVP with auth, customer records, tasks, and activity history.\n\nε░é\n\nε░âRefactor the billing module and reduce duplicate service logic.\n\nε░é\n\nε░âAdd role-based access control to this app.\n\nε░é\n\nε░âFind why the checkout flow intermittently fails and fix it.\n\nε░éYou do not need to say:\n\nΓÇ£use the architectΓÇ¥\n\nΓÇ£use the analystΓÇ¥\n\nΓÇ£switch to QAΓÇ¥\n\nThe orchestrator should infer that.\n\n\n\nPart 8: If you want BMAD loaded from one single path only\n\nThis is the honest answer:\n\nNot fully, by itself.\n\nA central BMAD folder like ~/.bmad-method/ is excellent as your source repository, but CopilotΓÇÖs supported always-on mechanisms are still the files it scans in recognized locations such as:\n\n~/.copilot/agents\n\n~/.copilot/instructions\n\nrepo .github/copilot-instructions.md\n\nrepo .github/instructions/*.instructions.md\n\nAGENTS.md in workspace roots (Visual Studio Code)\n\nSo the correct pattern is:\n\nRecommended pattern\n\nkeep the full BMAD package in ~/.bmad-method/\n\nkeep a thin global agent file in ~/.copilot/agents\n\nkeep thin global BMAD rules in ~/.copilot/instructions\n\nkeep repo-specific files inside each project\n\nThat is the most reliable setup.\n\n\n\nPart 9: If you want one-click rollout to all your future repos\n\nCreate a starter repository template with:\n\nε░â.github/\n\n  copilot-instructions.md\n\n  instructions/\n\n    react.instructions.md\n\n    backend.instructions.md\n\nAGENTS.md\n\ndocs/\n\n  architecture.md\n\n  requirements.md\n\nε░éThen every new project starts from that template.\n\nYou can also keep your user-level BMAD orchestrator in ~/.copilot/agents, so it is available everywhere regardless of repo. VS Code supports user-level and workspace-level custom agents separately. (Visual Studio Code)\n\n\n\nPart 10: A more advanced version using AGENTS.md\n\nIn each repo root, optionally add:\n\nε░âAGENTS.md\n\nε░éVS Code recognizes AGENTS.md as always-on instructions and explicitly recommends it when multiple AI agents work in the workspace. (Visual Studio Code)\n\nExample:\n\nε░â# AGENTS.md\n\n\n\nThis repository uses BMAD orchestration by default.\n\n\n\nOperating rules:\n\n- Start by classifying the task into analysis, architecture, implementation, or QA.\n\n- Use the smallest sufficient BMAD workflow.\n\n- Keep outputs traceable from request to implementation.\n\n- For medium or large work, produce a mini-plan before changing files.\n\n- Do not introduce architectural changes silently.\n\n- Always define validation steps.\n\nε░éThis helps when multiple agents or models are used in the same project.\n\n\n\nPart 11: Your exact target behavior\n\nWhat you want is best implemented like this:\n\nFinal working setup\n\nInstall BMAD centrally\n\n~/.bmad-method/\n\nCreate one global orchestrator\n\n~/.copilot/agents/bmad-orchestrator.agent.md\n\nCreate one global BMAD instruction file\n\n~/.copilot/instructions/00-bmad-global.instructions.md\n\nAdd repo instructions in each project\n\n.github/copilot-instructions.md\n\nOptionally add stack-specific rules\n\n.github/instructions/*.instructions.md\n\nIn VS Code chat\n\nchoose model: Claude Sonnet 4.6 or GPT-5.3 Codex\n\nchoose agent: bmad-orchestrator\n\nThat will make whichever model you use behave in BMAD style, with automatic internal role routing.\n\n\n\nPart 12: Limitations you should know\n\nA few things will still be true:\n\ncustom instructions generally affect chat requests, not normal inline autocomplete typing in the editor (Visual Studio Code)\n\nreal cross-agent handoffs in VS Code are supported, but they are still a visible workflow concept rather than a completely hidden runtime router (Visual Studio Code)\n\nrepository instructions can override or refine behavior, while user-level personal instructions have higher priority than repo-level ones (Visual Studio Code)\n\nSo this setup is extremely close to what you want, but the most reliable version is still one orchestrator agent that internally performs BMAD routing.\n\n\n\nFast checklist\n\nCreate these exact paths:\n\nε░â~/.bmad-method/\n\n~/.copilot/agents/bmad-orchestrator.agent.md\n\n~/.copilot/instructions/00-bmad-global.instructions.md\n\n<each-repo>/.github/copilot-instructions.md\n\n<each-repo>/.github/instructions/react.instructions.md\n\n<each-repo>/.github/instructions/backend.instructions.md\n\n<each-repo>/AGENTS.md\n\nε░éThen in VS Code:\n\nrestart\n\nopen Copilot Chat\n\nselect your model\n\nselect bmad-orchestrator\n\nstart chatting normally\n\nIf you want, I can turn this into a copy-paste BMAD starter pack with all file contents for a TypeScript/React + API project.\n\n\n\n',NULL,4,'2026-03-24 14:42:28.482294');
